# 73. Security Requirements

Teacher UI must never rely on frontend authorization alone.

Backend must verify:

```text
User
 ↓
Organization
 ↓
Course ownership
 ↓
Lesson permissions
 ↓
Action permission
```

Examples:

```text
Can this teacher edit this lesson?
Can this teacher publish this course?
Can this teacher view this student's data?
```

All sensitive operations require server-side authorization.

---

# 74. Unsaved Changes Protection

When leaving an editing screen:

```text
Unsaved Changes

You have changes that haven't been saved.

[Stay]
[Save & Leave]
[Leave Without Saving]
```

Autosave should reduce the frequency of this dialog.

---

# 75. Teacher UX Principles

The teacher dashboard should follow these principles:

### Automation first

AI should reduce repetitive work.

### Human control

Teachers must be able to review and modify generated content.

### Transparency

Show what AI generated and what the teacher changed.

### Recoverability

Drafts and versions should protect teacher work.

### Fast feedback

Processing and saving states should always be visible.

### Student-first publishing

Preview should reproduce the student experience.

---

# 76. Central AI Review Principle

AILPG should not treat AI output as automatically final.

The intended workflow is:

```text
AI Generates
      ↓
Teacher Reviews
      ↓
Teacher Edits
      ↓
Teacher Approves
      ↓
Student Receives
```

This creates a controlled human-in-the-loop learning-content workflow.

